      "id": "STU-001",
      "location": "src/gpt/liturgy/roman-rite/1962/propers/temporal/67-resumed-sixth-after-epiphany/sections/20-commentary.tex:47; sections/40-whole-person-and-desire.tex:23 and :47",
      "problem": "A recurring register pattern explains the writer's interpretive precautions or credits a text with the study's own qualification instead of stating the supported conclusion directly. At 20-commentary.tex:47, 'The connection can be developed without making the offering an assertion that the worshipper no longer needs help' narrates how to develop the connection and rejects an assertion no source has made. At 40-whole-person-and-desire.tex:23, 'The present interpretation uses Jerome's offered spiritual senses because its question concerns the Gospel's reach within a person' and 'It does not need to claim that the ordinary description of a large quantity has ceased to make sense' narrate the choice of emphasis and the interpretation's restraint after the actual difference has already been stated. At :47, 'The Gradual contributes a further qualification' makes the chant the agent of the study's qualification; the qualification comes from reading the full psalm beside this interpretation of interior reform. These instances recur across the commentary and second interpretation, meeting the packet's blocking-pattern threshold under guidance/editorial.md, 'State the finding, not the process that produced it' and 'Say what the texts say, plainly'.",
      "repair_target": "study",
      "required_result": "Revise this pattern into direct argument while retaining all substantive qualifications, source roles and differences between Chrysostom and Jerome. At 20-commentary.tex:47, a plain replacement for the methodological sentence and its repetitive continuation is: 'As the worshipper presents gifts received from God, he asks for mercy.' Keep the preceding Schuster attribution and citation. At 40-whole-person-and-desire.tex:23, the distinction can be stated directly: 'Chrysostom takes the three measures as a large quantity of meal; Jerome offers spiritual interpretations in which the Gospel brings the person's faculties into agreement.' Retain any further explanation needed for that actual distinction, but remove the narration of why this study chose an emphasis and what it need not claim. At :47, begin with the source fact and mark the inference in ordinary language, for example: 'The full psalm describes suffering endured without abandoning God's covenant. Faithfulness can therefore coexist with lament and outward injury.' Preserve the distinction between the appointed Gradual and its full psalm context, and preserve the pastoral qualification about suffering. Check nearby connecting sentences for the same pattern; do not turn the study's inference into a Father's claim or delete necessary claim-local qualifications such as the source-role distinction at :53. The repaired passages should teach the supported connection without describing the editorial precaution that produced it.",
      "severity": "blocking"
    }
  ],
